\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Strong zloty behind Polish debt improvement-Fitch.  \b0\par\par\uc2 556 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 2004\par 14:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By Karolina Slowikowska\par     WARSAW, Dec 10 (Reuters) - Poland's improving debt and\par deficit figures are due mainly to the zloty's gains and not the\par underlying fiscal picture which is key for the country's credit\par rating, Fitch Ratings said on Friday.\par     Fitch's main Poland analyst Ed Parker added the zloty is not\par overvalued at current rates and should not harm the EU\par newcomer's economic growth, which he sees at a healthy level of\par about 5 percent next year.\par     Despite progress on fiscal reforms this year, and a fall in\par the public debt burden to below 51 percent of GDP, Parker said\par he maintained his view that Poland's euro adoption might be\par delayed beyond 2010 without further reform of public finances.\par     "There has been little change in recent months and the\par improvement of the budget position has been mainly helped by the\par strong zloty," Parker told Reuters in an interview.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     "We have a stable outlook that provides a signal that the\par degree of fiscal consolidation is key for the rating."\par     All three main ratings agencies have punished Poland for\par dragging its feet on reforms of its bloated public sector with\par ratings or outlook downgrades over the past 18 months.\par     In May Fitch cut Poland's local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rating to "A" from\par "A-plus" and shifted its foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  outlook to stable from\par positive.\par     Since then the government has pushed through more of Economy\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Minister Jerzy Hausner's reforms and a 2005 budget which lowers  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 its general government deficit to 3.9 percent of GDP, still well  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 above the euro zone's 3 percent entry requirement.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Parker said the fall in the 2005 deficit to 35 billion  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 zlotys ($11.1 billion) is a step in the right direction, but  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 that much more is needed to make good on the current target of  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 euro entry in 2009.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     "I don't see enough progress here to join the euro zone this\par decade. There are many barriers to overcome," he said.\par  \par     ZLOTY, GROWTH HEALTHY\par     Parker said the zloty was not "overstrong" after it gained\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 15 percent this year and the current levels should not hurt  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Poland's largely export-driven economic recovery, despite a  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 slowdown in the third quarter.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Statistical office data on Thursday showed growth slowed to  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4.8 percent from 6.1 percent in the previous quarter as an  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 inflation spike dented consumer spending and a long-awaited  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 investment recovery proved slow in coming.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Poland's economy will grow at around 5 percent next year,  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 which is a healthy and sustainable level. It's not a slowdown  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 really, just a cyclical change. Investment should pick-up,"  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Parker said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Parker also said it is much too early to start a debate  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 about the zloty's entry rate for the pre-euro exchange rate  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 mechanism (ERM-2) and that he saw no possibility of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Comments by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and government officials last week  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 suggested they could discuss moves to weaken the zloty, although  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ministers have since hurried to reassure markets no immediate  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is in prospect.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's governor Leszek Balcerowicz is firmly  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and he is a powerful player. I see no  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for now," said Parker.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Poland\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has a floating rate and so the zloty can take care  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of itself," he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041210e0ca0086i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
