\par\par \b \uc2 FOCUS: Poland Rate Decision To Set Tone For Markets   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Hope   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 781 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juli 2004\par 13:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 WARSAW (Dow Jones)--Whether or not Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises rates Wednesday, its monetary policy decision will serve as a bellwether for markets in the months to come.   \par \par \uc2 Economists surveyed this week were split on whether we'll see a decision to stand pat or to lift the country's key interest rate this week. \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's key rate is called the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate. After a 50-basis-point hike last month, it's at 5.75%.   \par \par \uc2 "It will be a very close shave this time, given the declarations to control inflation," and the expectations for July CPI, SG Bank Economist Marcin Mroz said.   \par \par \uc2 Economists are, though, unified in saying that if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has any hope of hitting its year-end inflation target, its monetary policy council will have to raise key interest rates by 50-100 basis points this year.   \par \par \uc2 "Whatever the decision, it doesn't change where we'll be in six months. The question is whether they will go there gradually or more aggressively," Danske Bank senior analyst Lars Christensen said   \par \par \uc2 If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't hike rates Wednesday, some economists said they'd expect to see it happen in either August or September.   \par \par \uc2 The policy-making body saw a large amount of turnover from the latter part of 2003 until February of this year. To many in the markets, it's still not clear how hawkish or dovish the country's rate-setting body is.   \par \par \uc2 "This is one of our key issues for the moment. The jury is still out," ABN AMRO chief economist in London Zsolt Papp said. "How in the medium term can we determine if this group is as hawkish as the last one?"   \par \par \uc2 July's meeting follows one in June that resulted in a higher-than-expected rate hike. That 50-basis-point rise in June - markets were looking for a 25-basis-point rise - came after months of comments from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials about subtle changes in "degrees of caution," but no rate moves.   \par \par \uc2 If last month's surprise rate hike was any indication, the zloty stands to gain most from another rate hike Wednesday. Treasury bond yields would also follow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher.   \par \par \uc2 Since the last RPP meeting the zloty has climbed to PLZ3.6450 from PLZ3.7470 to the dollar on June 30 and to PLZ4.4360 versus the euro from PLZ4.5422 on June 30.   \par \par \uc2 Two year T-bond yields are trading at about 7.93% versus 7.53% on June 30, and five-year T-bond yields are at 7.98% versus 7.44% on June 30.   \par \par \uc2 If there is no rate hike, the zloty should stay stable until August's meeting, when a rate hike would be very likely.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy-making body said it needed to make its 50-point move on June 30 because of unexpectedly high inflation in May. That was a result of food price increases after Poland's May 1 entry into the European Union.   \par \par \uc2 And inflationary pressure continues. Economists expect July consumer price inflation between 4.7% and 5.0%. The policy-making body's end-2004 inflation target is 1.5% to 3.5%. June CPI was up 4.3% year-on-year, versus 3.4% in May, 2.2% in April and 1.7% in March.   \par \par \uc2 After making such a big move in June, some economists who spoke with Dow Jones this week said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely wait for July CPI results before raising rates again.   \par \par \uc2 Food and beverage prices in the first half of July were unchanged from the first half of June, meaning the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably needs more evidence of inflation before it acts again, said one economist.   \par \par \uc2 "Now there is less than a 50% chance so soon after the first increase," BRE Bank Economist Janusz Jankowiak said. "They'll raise by 50 points in August, but will wait for clear picture about July first."   \par \par \uc2 Those who forecast a rate rise on Wednesday say they expect the young policy-making body to want to be seen by markets as acting decisively on inflation.   \par \par \uc2 "They'll raise rates now by 50 points to send a strong message to the markets that they have inflation under control, even ahead of what is likely to be lower inflation in August," Bank BZ WBK Economist Piotr Bujak said.   \par \par \uc2 The policy meeting starts today and resumes on Wednesday, with a rate decision due around 1100 GMT Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Andrew Hope; Dow Jones Newswires, +4822 622-2767; andrew.hope@dowjones.com \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 27-07-04 1152GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040727e07r000kq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
