\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Polish FinMin-planned  \highlight22\uc2 fx \highlight\uc2  buying no  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 .   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Marta Karpinska and Wojciech Moskwa   \par \uc2 557 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2003\par 18:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 WARSAW, Dec 5 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry announced plans on Friday to exchange 250 million euros into zlotys on the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this month but vowed its venture into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading was not an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The ministry said the transaction reflected the need to exchange into zlotys about half of a 500 million euro ($603 million) loan Poland gained from the European Investment Bank.   \par \par \uc2 The ministry moved to assure markets that it fully backed Poland's free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The announcement of the deal was made to forestall suspicions the ministry was covertly playing the market to bolster the recently weak zloty, it said.   \par \par \uc2 There are several benefits for Poland if the zloty appreciates this year, such as boosting the budget's cash reserves and helping to keep key debt ratios in check.   \par \par \uc2 The rise in the zloty means Poland's public debt to gross domestic product ratio will fall because part of the debt is denominated in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "This transaction should not be treated as a market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it is just another way of gaining zlotys without having to go through the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Jacek Tomorowicz, the head of the finance ministry's foreign department, told Reuters.   \par \par \uc2 "We did not want to surprise the market so we revealed our intentions," said Tomorowicz, adding that markets should not view the announcement as a shift on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime thinking.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the transaction could well strengthen the zloty, but was not to be seen as a parallel to the Hungary where authorities have shown in past weeks they were ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and hike up interest rates to defend the weak forint.   \par \par \uc2 "As long as the ministry makes sure to be perceived as a client and not a market player, markets should treat such transactions as fairly natural and its credibility should not suffer," said Jacek Wisniewski, analyst at Bank Pekao.   \par \par \uc2 The zloty inched up in the wake of the announcement but then backtracked gains to finish the day below morning highs. At 1535 GMT the zloty traded at 4.646 to the euro versus last week's all-time lows against the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around 4.71.   \par \par \uc2 CONTAGION CONTAINED   \par \par \uc2 The transaction could also stabilise the market as volumes thin out towards the end of the year, helping to contain any potential spillover from still turbulent Hungarian markets. "Right now, particularly towards the end of the year, the market needs a trend setter and awareness that these flows will be going through the market will definitely prop up the zloty," said Marcin Mroz, chief economist at SG Bank in Warsaw.   \par \par \uc2 Early this year the ministry said it was ironing out ways it can buy or sell zlotys on the interbank market, if it felt such a transaction would benefit the budget or the market.   \par \par \uc2 Since January, the zloty has weakened by 14 percent versus the euro, while holding steady versus the U.S. dollar. This has helped exporters, but raised worries over the increasing costs of servicing foreign debt and key public debt to GDP ratios.   \par \par \uc2 If public debt - previously seen at around 53 percent of gross domestic product in 2003 - tops 55 percent, Poland will be forced to take punitive spending cuts which could destabilise public finances and economic growth.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031205dzc5002vz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
