\par\par \b \uc2 IMF: No Room For Polish Rate Cuts In Coming Months   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,073 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 November 2003\par 14:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Warsaw (Dow Jones)--Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no room to cut interest rates in coming months, the International Monetary Fund said Thursday, despite market expectations of a cut when the current rate-setting Monetary Policy Council, or RPP, is replaced in January.   \par \par \uc2 Instead, the IMF urged Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to focus on preventing an inflation rebound as the economy continues to recover. It should also retain its cornerstone policy of a free-floating zloty, the IMF said, in a statement summarizing its preliminary conclusions from a mission to \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "Preserving low inflation and non-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market should be the highest priority for the new RPP," the statement said.   \par \par \uc2 In recent days, Polish government officials have hinted at a possible return to a managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime based on a euro parity rate and a fluctuation band similar to the +/- 15% corridor set out of in European Union's ERMII exchange rate mechanism.   \par \par \uc2 However, any such intention was vigorously denied by Finance Minister Andrzej Raczko earlier this week.   \par \par \uc2 The IMF said Poland's parliament needs to quickly approve the government's plan to rein in its ballooning fiscal deficit in 2004-2007 through cuts in defense, administrative, and welfare spending.   \par \par \uc2 The plan, devised by Deputy Prime Minister Jerzy Hausner, has been heavily criticized by the parliamentary opposition and many members of the ruling Left Democratic Alliance.   \par \par \uc2 "Time is of the essence, however...We urge speedy consideration of all parts of the (Hausner) program," the IMF statement said.   \par \par \uc2 It called for a speeding-up of Poland's privatization effort, in part to help reduce the need for borrowing to finance fiscal deficits in coming years.   \par \par \uc2 The IMF forecast Poland's economy would register full-year GDP growth of 3.25% this year, rising to 4.25% in 2004. The latter projection is slightly below the government's projection of 5% annual growth.   \par \par \uc2 "In the current setting, where a recovery is in progress, a large fiscal stimulus is looming, and the yield curve is steepening, the scope in coming months for any further easing of monetary policy has largely disappeared," the IMF said.   \par \par \uc2 Warsaw (Dow Jones)--Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no room to cut interest rates in coming months, the International Monetary Fund said Thursday, despite market expectations of a cut when the current rate-setting Monetary Policy Council, or RPP, is replaced in January.   \par \par \uc2 Instead, the IMF urged Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to focus on preventing an inflation rebound as the economy continues to recover. It should also retain its cornerstone policy of a free-floating zloty, the IMF said, in a statement summarizing its preliminary conclusions from a mission to \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "Preserving low inflation and non-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market should be the highest priority for the new RPP," the statement said.   \par \par \uc2 In recent days, Polish government officials have hinted at a possible return to a managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime based on a euro parity rate and a fluctuation band similar to the +/- 15% corridor set out of in European Union's ERMII exchange rate mechanism.   \par \par \uc2 However, any such intention was vigorously denied by Finance Minister Andrzej Raczko earlier this week.   \par \par \uc2 The IMF said Poland's parliament needs to quickly approve the government's plan to rein in its ballooning fiscal deficit in 2004-2007 through cuts in defense, administrative, and welfare spending.   \par \par \uc2 The plan, devised by Deputy Prime Minister Jerzy Hausner, has been heavily criticized by the parliamentary opposition and many members of the ruling Left Democratic Alliance.   \par \par \uc2 "Time is of the essence, however...We urge speedy consideration of all parts of the (Hausner) program," the IMF statement said.   \par \par \uc2 It called for a speeding-up of Poland's privatization effort, in part to help reduce the need for borrowing to finance fiscal deficits in coming years.   \par \par \uc2 The IMF forecast Poland's economy would register full-year GDP growth of 3.25% this year, rising to 4.25% in 2004. The latter projection is slightly below the government's projection of 5% annual growth.   \par \par \uc2 "In the current setting, where a recovery is in progress, a large fiscal stimulus is looming, and the yield curve is steepening, the scope in coming months for any further easing of monetary policy has largely disappeared," the IMF said.   \par \par \uc2 The IMF said Poland was at an important juncture; recovery led by strong exports and domestic consumption couldn't be sustained under the continued burden of the bloated public sector.   \par \par \uc2 "Large and poorly addressed social transfers, inefficient public enterprises, and high and rising public debt are challenges Poland must rise to," the statement read.   \par \par \uc2 Spending cuts and consolidation of public finances proposed in Hausner's plan are aimed to bring the fiscal deficit to 2.1% of gross domestic product by 2007 from the 5.0% planned for 2004.   \par \par \uc2 IMF said that the government's privatization effort was unimpressive, leaving almost untapped a major source of non-debt budget financing.   \par \par \uc2 "In our conversations with the Treasury Minister we strongly emphasized the necessity to speed up privatization," head of IMF mission Susan Schadler said.   \par \par \uc2 Schadler said Poland would sustain low inflation rate by the end of this year and envisaged a moderate pickup in the inflation rate in 2004.   \par \par \uc2 "Year-end inflation in 2003 will come at 1.5%. A weaker zloty and picking up recovery will drive inflation up to just below 3.0% at the end of 2004," Schadler said.   \par \par \uc2 In October, annual inflation rose to 1.2% from 0.9% in September.   \par \par \uc2 Commenting on the IMF assessment Finance Minister Andrzej Raczko said the government was fully committed to a timely implementation of fiscal reform.   \par \par \uc2 Raczko stressed the main focus of the plan was to bring Poland's rising fiscal deficit and public debt in line with levels required for the adoption of the euro.   \par \par \uc2 He said the government didn't intend to tap into the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  provisions to trim the budget deficit.   \par \par \uc2 "We can't count on miraculous solutions such as untying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves. We have to think in terms of sustainable trimming of the deficit," Raczko said.   \par \par \uc2 Poland's former Finance Minister Grzegorz Kolodko wanted to release some 9 billion zlotys ($1=PLN3.8835) of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  provisions to improve the budget balance.   \par \par \uc2 -By Marek Strzelecki; Dow Jones Newswires, +4822 622 2767; marek.strzelecki@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031120dzbk000re\par }\page {\par\fs2