\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-SNB's Roth - ready to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  to stop franc gains. \b0\par\par\uc2 263 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 2003\par 10:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, June 18 (Reuters) - Further appreciation by the Swiss franc could have dire consequences for Switzerland's economy and force the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Swiss National Bank Chairman Jean-Pierre Roth said. \par \par \uc2  "If again, as a result of global political uncertainty, the franc becomes a safe haven for investors, we will have no other choice but to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Roth told \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Rzeczpospolita daily in an interview on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Roth was in Warsaw on Monday. The interview was published in Polish and translated into English by Reuters. \par \par \uc2  "Things have gone far enough and further appreciation in the current situation could have very negative consequences for the economy," the daily quoted Roth as saying. \par \par \uc2  Roth said the SNB last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in 1995 in a co-ordinated effort with other central banks to boost the value of the dollar. The SNB's last unilateral market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took place in the early 1980s. \par \par \uc2  The SNB, which keeps interest rates 150-200 basis points below those in the euro zone, last week vowed to push beyond the limits of traditional monetary policy to foster growth for the recession-hit, export-dependent Swiss economy. \par \par \uc2  Asked about the risk of deflation, Roth said: "With inflation at 0.4 percent there is a danger that some negative event would bring about a fall in consumer prices. But the real danger for us would be franc appreciation, which could be regarded as deflation of imported goods." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020030618dz6i00gde\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
