\par\par \b \uc2  Polish Ctrl Bk: Doesn't Rule Out Zloty Trading Band \b0\par\par\uc2 675 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2002\par 16:10\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  WARSAW -(Dow Jones)- Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Thursday that it isn't ruling out the possibility of reintroducing a trading band for the free-floating zloty, but a senior bank official said later the reference was to the long-planned adoption of the European Union's ERM II \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  mechanism after 2004. \par \par \uc2  In a statement, the bank said its inflation-targeting strategy ruled out adopting a specific exchange-rate goal, but added "it wouldn't exclude the possibility that this strategy is compatible with introducing a wide \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuation band around a market-derived central parity rate." \par \par \uc2  The statement appeared to be in response to a suggestion from Deputy Finance Minister Ryszard Michalski earlier Thursday that Poland might return the zloty to a managed, euro-linked float even before it enters the ERM II - a step expected soon after Poland joins the E.U. in mid-2004. \par \par \uc2  But senior central banker Grzegorz Wojtowicz, a member of the policy-setting Monetary Policy Council, denied there were any plans to drop the zloty free float before joining ERM II. \par \par \uc2  "All we're saying is that a direct inflation target can be reconciled with the process of adopting the euro," Wojtowicz told Dow Jones Newswires in a telephone interview. \par \par \uc2  "Introducing a trading band before joining ERM II would be irresponsible, because the zloty could break through the band at any moment. But once we're in ERM II and have a euro-zloty parity rate agreed with the (European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ), then both sides would have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect the band," he said. \par \par \uc2  Since June, \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the finance ministry have been trying to hammer out a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy for the potentially volatile period preceding a direct linkup with the euro under ERM II. The ERM II mechanism would set a central parity rate for the zloty against the euro, bounded by a +/- 15% trading band. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly stressed that it sees no  reason to abandon the present \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime - based since  April 2000 on a free-floating zloty - until Poland enters ERM II. \par \par \uc2  But finance ministry officials have urged an earlier return to a zloty trading band, perhaps accompanied by a devaluation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's central parity rate. They say both moves would help reverse the zloty's current appreciation trend, boosting the competitiveness of Polish exports and reviving economic growth. \par \par \uc2  "We think the objective should be an exchange rate more  favorable to exporters," Deputy Finance Minister Michalski, co-chairman of the government-\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  working group on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy, told the Polish Press Agency in an interview Thursday. \par \par \uc2  But Michalski's main negotiating partner, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Deputy President Andrzej Bratkowski, repeated Thursday that weakening the zloty is an unacceptable argument for any change in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime. \par \par \uc2  "We're ready to listen to the finance ministry's arguments and we don't exclude - at least theoretically - the early introduction of a trading band," Bratkowski told Dow Jones Newswires. "But if the goal is simply to weaken the zloty against its underlying trend, this is something we'd definitely oppose." \par \par \uc2  Bratkowski said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might accept the zloty's return to a managed float if the finance ministry demonstrates the move is needed to reduce volatility in the run-up to ERM II entry. \par \par \uc2  "One could imagine early introduction of a trading band  for psychological purposes, to stabilize the market," he said. "But in the present low-inflation environment, we're not expecting big exchange-rate fluctuations." \par \par \uc2  However, Michalski's remarks Thursday indicated the finance ministry is looking beyond the term of the current Monetary Policy Council, which expires in January 2004. \par \par \uc2  "The more rigorously the current Council sticks to conservative monetary policies, the more likely we'll see more innovative approach in the new Council...A lot can happen over the next (18) months," Michalski told PAP. \par \par \uc2  -By David McQuaid and Malgorzata Halaba; Dow Jones Newswires, +4822 622 2766; david.mcquaid@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020021205dyc5000yh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
