\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW-Polish c.bank targets euro compliance 2005. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Marta Karpinska \par \uc2 672 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2002\par 15:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, Dec 3 (Reuters) - Poland is on course to meet entry targets for joining the euro zone by 2005, the year after it expects to join the European Union, a top central banker said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Deputy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Andrzej Bratkowski told Reuters the chances that Poland would meet tough euro-zone qualification targets were actually improving amid a faster-than-expected recovery. \par \par \uc2  The comments by Bratkowski, whose views reflect the thinking of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Leszek Balcerowicz, came after Finance Minister Grzegorz Kolodko sought to cool expectations of an early \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  switch. \par \par \uc2  "I think we will be ready in 2005, and if I had to weigh our chances I would put them at around 90 percent now," Bratkowski said in an interview. \par \par \uc2  Poland is the largest of 10 candidates hoping to close EU entry talks soon and adopt the euro after spending two years or more in the Exchange Rate Mechanism (ERM-2), a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilisation regime. \par \par \uc2  Bratkowski said monetary policy must remain cautious and fiscal policy tight to ensure that Poland, having won the battle against inflation, keeps prices under control as it sets its sights on euroland. \par \par \uc2  "Next year and the year after we will have higher economic growth coupled with extra budget expenditures related to EU entry, which will create inflationary pressure," he said. \par \par \uc2  "That, plus uncertainty on whether planned fiscal reforms will be sound, will definitely keep monetary policy on the cautious side." \par \par \uc2  Bratkowski, who is not a member of the rate-setting Monetary Policy Council, said it was difficult to gauge whether interest rates were already where they should be given Poland's long-term growth prospects, but said some more easing was still possible. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slashed more than twelve percentage points off its main market rate since early 2001. It eased last month by a cautious 25 basis points to 6.75 percent, leaving the main rate still far above inflation of just over one percent. \par \par \uc2  NO \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  Like other central European countries, \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  is battling to curb the strength of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as "convergence" investors bet that bond yields will fall towards euro-zone levels and the zloty will rise as Poland's economy catches up with richer western Europe. \par \par \uc2  But Bratkowski said there was no need for Poland to abandon its hands-off policy on the zloty , adding it was "difficult to imagine" circumstances which would force \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the free-floating unit. \par \par \uc2  "A country which does not move in to protect the exchange rate is the least likely to be forced to do so," he said. \par \par \uc2  He said further strengthening of the zloty was inevitable, but the trick was to set a credible central parity for the zloty's entry into the ERM-2 which would anchor the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a 30 percent fluctuation band. \par \par \uc2  "The key will be to negotiate with the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) a parity rate which would be believable for the markets and which will allow the economy to grow," he added. \par \par \uc2  Bratkowski countered calls by ECB and EU officials for candidates not to rush into the euro zone by saying the so-called Maastricht criteria - which include low inflation and low state borrowing - did not rule out strong growth. \par \par \uc2  "I simply do not believe that those two targets of entering the euro-zone quickly and maintaining high economic growth are contradictory," Bratkowski said. \par \par \uc2  "But I do think that we should try to stay in the ERM-2 for as little time as possible, maybe less than two years, if the ECB agrees. \par \par \uc2  "The final euro-zone accession date is a technical issue, but we do want to be compliant with Maastricht criteria in 2005," Bratkowski said. \par \par \uc2  ECB governing council member Jean-Claude Trichet on Monday warned against admitting countries into the euro zone which were not ready for membership, while Economic Affairs Commissioner Pedro Solbes predicted that at most only one or two applicant countries would be zone members by 2007. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021203dyc300d4e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
