\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Rosati says zloty too strong, but may remain firm. \b0\par\par\uc2 383 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 November 2002\par 09:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  Adds more comments from Rosati, background \par \par \uc2  WARSAW, Nov 25 (Reuters) - Poland's zloty - which has rallied over the past month along with other central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  - is too strong given macroeconomic fundamentals, a member of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's interest rate-setting panel said on Monday. \par \par \uc2  "The main reason behind the recent rally in the zloty is the fading uncertainty over Poland's future European Union membership," Dariusz Rosati, one of 10 members of the Monetary Policy Council (MPC) told public radio. \par \par \uc2  "But generally speaking, the zloty is slightly too strong given the macroeconomic fundamentals," he added. \par \par \uc2  But Rosati brushed off expectations of a significant weakening of the zloty in months ahead as Poland appears firmly en route to join the European Union in 2004 and the eurozone later this decade. \par \par \uc2  "I think the strengthening of the zloty might continue. In a perspective of two-three-years, the zloty will remain a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Rosati - a dove on the 10-member MPC panel said. \par \par \uc2  "It is an illusion, on the part of the government or some businesses that it is possible to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a sustainable way," he added. \par \par \uc2  The zloty has gained six percent against the dollar and three versus the firmer euro over the last month, as fears surrounding Poland's EU accession have eased and foreign portfolio investors are increasingly betting on Polish asset prices quickly converging with those in the euro zone. \par \par \uc2  Such upward pressure has forced Hungary and Slovakia to cut interest rates in past weeks despite a far more uncertain inflation environment than \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's. Slovakia and the Czech Republic have also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to try cap their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' uptrend. \par \par \uc2  In an interview last week's with Reuters, Rosati said the zloty's strength was nevertheless not worrying enough to warrant \b \highlight22\uc2 forex interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  or changes to \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime. \par \par \uc2  The Polish government - which together with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decides on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime - has repeatedly said the zloty was overvalued and hurting struggling exporters. \par \par \uc2  Rosati would not comment on this week's policy meeting, due to make a rate decision on Wednesday, which most analysts expect to order a reduction in the main market rate of seven percent. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021125dybp00hfy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
