\par\par \b \uc2  WRAPUP 1-Polish c.bankers say mkts won't drive rate policy. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Justyna Pawlak \par \uc2 637 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2002\par 16:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, Nov 22 (Reuters) - Polish central bankers signalled on Friday they are not likely to follow their regional peers in axing interest rates to curb strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and suggested markets had gone too far in pricing in EU and euro-zone entry. Policymakers across eastern Europe are scrambling to ward off a flood of foreign money into local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as prospects brighten for eight of the region's states to join the wealthy European Union in 2004. \par \par \uc2  But Poland has so far stood by its hands-off \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy as other regional central banks slash rates or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength. \par \par \uc2  Setting \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  further apart from its peers, Monetary Policy Council (MPC) member Cezary Jozefiak warned that rate cuts may fail to dampen foreign appetitate for local assets, which yield much more than their euro zone equivalents. \par \par \uc2  "I have doubts whether changing the interest rate disparity (between eastern Europe and the euro zone) will help," Jozefiak told Reuters in an interview. \par \par \uc2  Jozefiak also reiterated his long-standing views, shared by many of the MPC's 10 members, that market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the zloty was not a good policy solution in a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime. \par \par \uc2  The zloty firmed on his comments, seen in the market as an assurance that no administrative steps would be taken to stem foreign inflows. Bonds markets were unruffled, continuing to price in a modest rate cut next week. \par \par \uc2  Twelve out of 20 analysts polled by Reuters on Friday said the modest pace of recovery and near-record low inflation still left room for a cut of up to 50 basis points in Poland's main seven percent rate. \par \par \uc2  Comments supportive of easing on Thursday by MPC dove Dariusz Rosati led several analysts to change their minds and predict a modest easing next week rather than later. \par \par \uc2  The Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is meanwhile expected to cut by 50-100 basis points on Monday after last week's half-point cut, while the Czech and Slovak banks have both slashed rates lately. \par \par \uc2  BUDGET CONCERNS \par \par \uc2  Another hawkish MPC member joined forces with Warsaw's chief EU entry negotiator to warn that the government budget was at risk in the future, possibly increasing the risk premiums demanded by foreign investors on zloty assets. \par \par \uc2  Boguslaw Grabowski said Poland risked missing its timetable to meet the Maastricht treaty criteria on economic and monetary convergence with the euro zone by 2005 - the target date agreed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the government. \par \par \uc2  "I do see a high risk of a delay to the 2005 timetable for meeting the Maastricht criteria in particular the three percent budget deficit to GDP target," he told reporters in London. \par \par \uc2  Poland has to cut its budget gap to three percent of gross domestic product to meet the euro entry criteria. Based on EU rules, the finance ministry forecasts a deficit of 3.9 percent next year. \par \par \uc2  A smaller deficit will require fast revenue-boosting growth and deep spending cuts, which will be hard to sell to voters before a referendum on joining the EU next year. \par \par \uc2  EU negotiator Jan Truszczynski fanned new worries over fiscal policy, saying Poland faced a budget crunch after EU entry if its financial entry terms are not improved. \par \par \uc2  "A big budget risk will materialise if no changes to our entry conditions are introduced. That is the risk of losing liquidity," Truszczynski told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Truszczynski said it was unfair for Poland to pay full membership dues without receiving full benefits of EU entry, especially in farming subsidies which are to start at 25 percent of what EU members receive and reach full aid in 10 years. \par \par \uc2  But he added the European Commission may sweeten entry proposal for farmers over the next few days. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021122dybm00t78\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
