\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Polish c.banker - fundamentals back strong zloty. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Pawel Kozlowski \par \uc2 442 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 November 2002\par 19:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  ZALESIE, Poland, Nov 21 (Reuters) - Poland's deputy \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Jerzy Stopyra said on Thursday the zloty's recent rally was "justified by fundamentals" and not driven only by speculative inflows. \par \par \uc2  His comments contradict recent statements from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government officials who call the rally, which has swept other central European markets, speculative. \par \par \uc2  "We do not see any speculation," Stopyra, who rarely speaks publicly about his views on the economy and deals mainly with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told reporters on the sidelines of a banking supervision seminar. \par \par \uc2  Foreign players are piling into the region as prospects brighten for many of the region's states to join the European Union in 2004 and later adopt the lower-yielding euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Such upward pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has forced Hungary and Slovakia to cut rates in past weeks despite a far-more uncertain inflation environment than \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's. Slovakia and the Czech Republic have also \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The zloty has risen three percent to the euro and six percent to the dollar over the last month. \par \par \uc2  Earlier on Thursday, member of the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Monetary Policy Council Dariusz Rosati said the zloty's rally was a "clear speculative inflow based on interest rate cut expectations." \par \par \uc2  But Polish central bankers still steer clear of any hints of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  even though they admit that they are begining to pay attention to the exchange rate as a factor affecting its policy since inflation stabilised at low levels. \par \par \uc2  Government officials have long demanded orchestrated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to curb the zloty and protect Polish exporters. \par \par \uc2  The comments from Stopyra, who advises the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor on all policy issues but has no direct say over exchange rate decisions, shows there is still a strong belief within the Polish national bank that the zloty should be allowed to run its course. \par \par \uc2  "If the market saw as positive the recent data about the Polish economy, including good growth in the third quarter, a very good situation on the current account, a moderate level of state deficit and low inflation, then it priced it in," he said. \par \par \uc2  Asked if he was concerned that the strong zloty could hurt the current account, Stopyra said: \par \par \uc2  "The market regulates itself. So even if the strong zloty worsens the trade situation, then the exchange rate will immediately fall." \par \par \uc2  Poland's economy is now slowly emerging from its deepest growth slowdown in more than a decade, which was driven by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hawkish anti-inflation policy, high government deficit and poor global performance. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021121dybl00yvo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
