\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Polish c.bank defends independence before debate. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wojciech Moskwa \par \uc2 703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2002\par 19:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, May 23 (Reuters) - Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  defended its independence on Thursday ahead of a crunch parliamentary debate where deputies were expected to back proposals to force it to cut interest rates to revive a weak economy. \par \par \uc2  After a months-long war of words between the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the largest party in the left-wing coalition was expected to set the legislative ball rolling by backing two amendments to the National Bank charter. \par \par \uc2  That should ensure a big majority for the proposals, which would require the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to consider jobs and growth - and not just prices - in setting policy. \par \par \uc2  They would also increase the size of the rate-setting Monetary Policy Council (MPC), enabling the panel to be packed with doves and creating a stable majority for easy money. \par \par \uc2  Opposing the changes, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Leszek Balcerowicz said a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  free of political interference was a vital part of any properly run country. \par \par \uc2  "In all well-constructed states of the world, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is independent so that it can protect citizens against inflation," Balcerowicz told reporters before the late-night debate, which was due to begin at 8 p.m. (1800 GMT). \par \par \uc2  "Removing the independence of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have an inflated price. And inflation does not lead to prosperity." \par \par \uc2  EU CONCERNS \par \par \uc2  Poland, the largest of 10 candidates in talks to join the European Union by 2004, needs to kick-start an economy languishing in its worst economic slowdown in a decade to ensure it can compete in the bloc's single marketplace. \par \par \uc2  But leaders worry that tampering with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  independence will annoy Brussels and make it harder for Poland to realise its longer-term goal of adopting the euro, managed by the independent European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Maybe by introducing these changes the Monetary Policy Council will realise it must increase its cooperation with the government," said Robert Smolen, spokesman of Prime Minister Leszek Miller's Democratic Left Alliance (SLD). \par \par \uc2  "The government must have a bigger say in the decisions made by the MPC. I do not think these bills go against EU norms and introduces changes which threaten the bank's independence." \par \par \uc2  LATE DEBATE \par \par \uc2  The Sejm lower house of parliament was expected to refer the proposals to committee for further work, meaning the threatened loss of independence will be hanging over the MPC when it holds its policy meeting next week. \par \par \uc2  The MPC, which has already halved rates since early 2001 to 9.5 percent, has said further cuts could jeopardise low inflation and risk inflating Poland's current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  It has also resisted government pressure to force down the zloty's exchange rate, with MPC member Boguslaw Grabowski saying Poland should stick to its free-floating regime in the run-up to joining the EU. \par \par \uc2  Grabowski also said any attempt to strip the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its joint role in setting \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy would violate Poland's constitution. \par \par \uc2  The heated rates-and-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate in Poland reflects a dilemma faced by other EU candidates in eastern Europe, such as Hungary and the Czech Republic, where \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation is putting economies under enormous pressure. \par \par \uc2  The appreciation is driven by investor bets that prices in candidate countries will align themselves with higher levels in the EU over time, threatening a fatal loss of competitiveness in communist-era industries such as steel and shipbuilding. \par \par \uc2  Poland's government has decided to regain control over debt-hit shipyard Stocznia Szczecin, in the first such renationalisation since the fall of communism in 1989. It says the strong zloty is a factor behind the yard's troubles. \par \par \uc2  PRESIDENT OPPOSES \par \par \uc2  President Aleksander Kwasniewski, a former SLD leader, has said he opposes any attack on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's independence. \par \par \uc2  But he is unlikely to risk his veto being overturned by a three-fifths majority. He could instead refer any legislation to the Constitutional Tribunal for a possibly lengthy review. \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund threw its weight behind the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the feud, saying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not the right answer to \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's poor policy balance, characterised by tight money and a loose fiscal stance. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020523dy5n00x6n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
