\par\par \b \uc2  Polish MPs to debate curbing c.bank independence. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Wojciech Moskwa \par \uc2 691 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Mai 2002\par 14:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, May 23 (Reuters) - Poland's lower house of parliament will on Thursday debate controversial proposals to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's independence which would effectively force it to cut interest rates and boost the weak economy. \par \par \uc2  After a months-long rhetorical war between the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the largest party in the left-wing coalition was expected to set the legislative ball rolling by backing two amendments to the National Bank charter. \par \par \uc2  That should ensure a big majority for the proposals, which would require the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to consider jobs and growth - and not just prices - in setting policy. \par \par \uc2  They would also increase the size of the rate-setting Monetary Policy Council (MPC), enabling the panel to be packed with doves and creating a stable majority for easy money. \par \par \uc2  Poland, the largest of 10 candidates in talks to join the European Union by 2004, needs to kick-start an economy languishing in its worst economic slowdown in a decade to ensure it can compete in the bloc's single marketplace. \par \par \uc2  But leaders worry that tampering with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  independence will annoy Brussels and make it harder for Poland to realise its longer-term goal of adopting the euro, managed by the independent European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Maybe by introducing these changes the Monetary Policy Council will realise it must increase its cooperation with the government," said Robert Smolen, spokesman of Prime Minister Leszek Miller's Democratic Left Alliance (SLD). \par \par \uc2  "The government must have a bigger say in the decisions made by the MPC. I do not think these bills go against EU norms and introduces changes which threaten the bank's independence." \par \par \uc2  LATE DEBATE \par \par \uc2  The Sejm lower house of parliament begins a late-night debate on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills at 8 p.m. (1800 GMT), and is expected to refer the proposals to committee for further work. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Leszek Balcerowicz, who will attend the sitting, can expect a rough reception from populists who have demonised him as the man to blame for the 17.8 percent unemployment rate - just short of a record. \par \par \uc2  The MPC, which has already halved rates since early 2001 to 9.5 percent, has said further cuts could jeopardise low inflation and risk inflating Poland's current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  It has also resisted government pressure to force down the zloty's exchange rate, saying there is no alternative to Poland's two-year-old free floating regime. \par \par \uc2  The heated rates-and-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate in Poland reflects a dilemma faced by other EU candidates in eastern Europe, such as Hungary and the Czech Republic, where \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation is putting economies under enormous pressure. \par \par \uc2  The appreciation is driven by investor bets that prices in candidate countries will align themselves with higher levels in the EU over time, threatening a fatal loss of export competitiveness in communist-era industries such as steel and shipbuilding. \par \par \uc2  Poland's government has decided to regain control over debt-hit shipyard Stocznia Szczecin, in the first such renationalisation since the fall of communism in 1989. It says the strong zloty is a factor behind the yard's troubles. \par \par \uc2  SNOWBALL EFFECT \par \par \uc2  Politicians say that once the bills go before the public finance committee, new proposals may be piled on to the measure, possibly further reducing the national bank's policy role. \par \par \uc2  One area could be the joint role of the government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in setting exchange rate policy, which could be changed to give politicians the upper hand. "We can't rule out such an option," the SLD's Smolen said. \par \par \uc2  President Aleksander Kwasniewski, a former SLD leader, has said he opposes any attack on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's independence. \par \par \uc2  But he is unlikely to risk his veto being overturned by a three-fifths majority. He could instead refer any legislation to the Constitutional Tribunal for a possibly lengthy review. \par \par \uc2  The International Monetary Fund on Thursday threw its weight behind the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the feud, saying \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not the right answer to \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's poor policy balance, characterised by tight money and a loose fiscal stance. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020523dy5n00nml\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
