\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Polish govt to talk to c.bank on strong zloty. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Justyna Pawlak \par \uc2 669 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Mai 2002\par 19:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, May 14 (Reuters) - Poland's left-wing government said on Tuesday it wanted to regulate the free floating zloty and would hold talks with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at containing the strength of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Speaking after a cabinet meeting, Finance Minister Marek Belka said the buoyant zloty was hurting exporters and risked causing an extended period of economic stagnation. \par \par \uc2  He said the government had asked his ministry to propose changes to Poland's two-year-old free \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  float aimed at preventing excessive appreciation. \par \par \uc2  But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which under Polish law must approve changes in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, poured cold water on any expectations that Poland's free float would be changed soon or that it might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the buoyant zloty. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the government was pnrobably hoping to create an explicit - or secret - trading band around the zloty that would require bankers to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the unit gets too strong. \par \par \uc2  Belka declined to map out his proposal, saying it would not be appropriate to speculate in public about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Exchange rate policy does not have to be publicly announced, to keep the (financial) market guessing," he said. \par \par \uc2  He did say he opposed fixing the zloty to a hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or reintroducing a crawling peg for the unit. \par \par \uc2  The zloty, which had fallen up to 1.5 percent earlier to a seven-month low versus the euro on pre-cabinet jitters, pared losses on hopes the government would refrain from legislative pressure in the talks with the bank. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  DIGS IN HEELS \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's top \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy official, deputy governor Andrzej Bratkowski, said no changes were needed in the short term, although Poland did need to prepare to adopt the euro \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after joining the European Union. \par \par \uc2  Poland will have to corral the unit into a 30-percentage point band for at least two years after joining the EU, expected in 2004, before it can adopt the euro later in the decade. \par \par \uc2  Hungary, which along with Poland and the Czech Republic is a top candidate for EU entry, created a trading band in line with EU's Exchange Rate Mechanism (ERM-2) while the Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the fully floated crown. \par \par \uc2  "There is no need for quick action," Bratkowski told Reuters. "If the main point (of talks government-\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  talks) would be to start \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  then an agreement is not likely." \par \par \uc2  Ministers blame the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's caution in easing its high interest rates for Poland's worst economic slowdown in a decade and drawing in speculative capital, forcing up the zloty and pricing exporters out of key foreign markets. \par \par \uc2  Their criticism was backed by the European Bank for Reconstruction and Development (EBRD) which said monetary policy had been "restrictive" and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had cut interest rates too slowly in response to a weak economy. \par \par \uc2  But, while still seeing scope to ease rates, the EBRD said in a report that political pressure on the National Bank of Poland might actually delay further rate cuts. \par \par \uc2  "The conduct of monetary policy has been complicated by statements of government members that could threaten the \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  independence of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," the EBRD said. \par \par \uc2  STRONG ZLOTY HELPS SOME \par \par \uc2  Results from eastern Europe's largest mobile phone company and oil refining business, meanwhile, showed that a strong zloty can actually help Polish firms which have borrowed heavily in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Both Polska Telefonia Cyfrowa, operator of the leading Era GSM brand, and oil group PKN Orlen blamed weaker-than-expected first quarter net profits on temporary weakness in the zloty, which forced up the cost of servicing their hard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debts. \par \par \uc2  Central bankers argue the zloty's strength is unavoidable because the government's hefty treasury issuance, and falling investment risk linked to prospects for \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's EU entry, will attract capital regardless of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  For a factbox on \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime click here:. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020514dy5e00u99\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
