\par\par \b \uc2  Hungary forint, zloty seen diverging on way to ERM. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sandor Peto \par \uc2 606 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Februar 2002\par 15:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  BUDAPEST, Feb 7 (Reuters) - Hungary's forint is likely to strengthen before it enters the European Exchange Rate Mechanism in 2004, despite a suggestion by Poland's finance minister that the zloty should be devalued before EU entry, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Marek Belka, stressing he was speaking only for himself, said late on Wednesday that the zloty could suffer speculative attack after Poland wraps up European Union (EU) accession talks, and advocated an "up-front" devaluation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "The zloty is regarded fundamentally overvalued now," said Gyorgy Kovacs of DZ Bank. "Everybody expects the forint to strengthen, while the best case for the zloty is stability." \par \par \uc2  A weaker unit ahead of Poland's entry into the Exchange Rate Mechanism (ERM-2) - which is designed to ensure \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability prior to entering the euro zone - would help bolster the economy. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Belka was trying to tackle a specific Polish problem of the worst economic slowdown in the largest future EU member, while other candidates in the region performed well. \par \par \uc2  "Belka is trying to ride a weak (bond and zloty) market sentiment and talk the zloty down to even lower levels," said Istvan Racz, regional analyst at CSFB in Budapest. "The economy is weak, unemployment high - the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an issue there." \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the largest candidate countries in Central Europe, which are set to close accession talks this year, surged in the past year, weighing on export performances amid a global slowdown. Hungary weathered it well, while Poland was worst hit. \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials warned last year that the candidates must meet the EU's strict inflation criteria but also need a pick-up in growth and wages - requirements which would may bring about exchange rate adjustment in the region. \par \par \uc2  DIFFERENT TRACKS, SAME DESTINATION \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Belka's words reflected the rhetoric of other ministers, who advocate a lower exchange rate to boost exports and move the economy out from stagnation. \par \par \uc2  The Czechs are making efforts to curb the surging crown and Hungary can also expect the forint to rise, but both economies are growing robustly at 3-4 percent rates. \par \par \uc2  Poland needs a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to kick-start its economy and currently only verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is possible as \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  must set up a parity rate for the zloty, which floats freely, if it wants it to devalue, analysts said. A Polish adjustment should be cautious as the applicants must enter the ERM with relatively stable exchange rates, while closing entry talks, a likely global recovery and new productivity gains could put upwards pressure on central European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  by next year, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "If you fix the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a weaker than justified level (in the ERM), its firming is blocked and this creates inflationary pressure," said Kovacs. \par \par \uc2  Hungary is one step ahead of Poland after adopting an ERM-type 30-percent forint flotation band last year. \par \par \uc2  The band will survive EU entry, but convergence-triggered capital inflows could lead the government to move the parity rate higher by some 10 percent next year so as to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  banging at the top of the band, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The forint is not overvalued, its purchasing power parity is well behind the euro, while its productivity disadvantage narrows," Racz said. "A forint at the top of the band is unpleasant for investors - there is no upside, only downside." The forint and Hungarian bonds weakened this week. The forint traded at 11 percent above parity on Wednesday, dragged down by Polish falls after rises on the wings of rallies in Poland early this year. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020207dy2700svz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
