\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Czechs' crown problem mirrored across region. \b0\par\par\uc2  By James Thornhill \par \uc2 823 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2002\par 16:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2002 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Jan 22 (Reuters) - Czech authorities have adopted a high risk strategy in trying to curb the crown's rise, highlighting a problem facing other emerging European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as EU accession triggers strong foreign investment flows. \par \par \uc2  The Czech National Bank (CNB) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday and cut interest rates by 25 basis points in an effort to curb the crown's sharp appreciation, but buyers soon returned to the market after an initial spike lower. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the CNB's move did little to address the fundamental reasons behind crown strength and, while it may hold up the crown's rise in the short term, it might also store up problems for a later date. \par \par \uc2  "If you ask whether this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in line with Czech fundamentals, the answer is no," said Callum Henderson, emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  Poland and Hungary, which like the Czech Republic are seen likely to join the European Union (EU) in 2004, are also facing problems posed by strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  However, analysts see \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a high-risk strategy, from which \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Hungary have shied away. \par \par \uc2  While authorities in some emerging markets would welcome hefty inflows of foreign investment, for the Czechs they are mainly causing headaches. \par \par \uc2  A deal between the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week arranged to lock away 25 billion Czech crowns ($683 million) in privatisation proceeds in the bank's foreign reserves until 2007. \par \par \uc2  "Even if all privatisation revenues are kept off the market this year, which will be difficult, the Czechs are still going to see strong portfolio inflows," Henderson said. \par \par \uc2  Yet with privatisation revenues of around 144 billion crowns due, and more expected to follow, few believe that the deal will be enough to stem the crown's steep ascent. \par \par \uc2  The Czech bond market also is expected to continue to attract capital inflows as its economy converges ahead of EU accession and interest rates fall towards euro zone levels. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  AS SMOOTHING MECHANISM \par \par \uc2  While many market players agree the authorities will not be able to stop the crown strengthening over the long term, some accept the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has at least managed to slow it down. \par \par \uc2  "One can assume that if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't there the (euro/crown) market would be trading much lower," said a senior dealer at a bank in London. \par \par \uc2  The amount of Monday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was estimated by traders at between 200-400 million euros, and with average daily turnover in the region of 450 million euros it's clear that those amounts can have a significant impact. \par \par \uc2  However, the dealer said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might be storing up trouble for the future if it continues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's short crown position is likely to lose money if, as even the authorities expect, the unit strengthens over the long term. This loss will have to be accounted for in the government budget, which is already deep in deficit. \par \par \uc2  Persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rate cuts to combat crown strength could also lead to inflationary pressure. \par \par \uc2  DIFFERENT APPROACH IN POLAND, HUNGARY \par \par \uc2  In some ways strong local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are more of a headache for Hungary and particularly Poland, because their economies aren't performing as well as in the Czech Republic. \par \par \uc2  But so far the authorities have limited their actions to verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and monetary easing. \par \par \uc2  "In \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has strongly committed to non \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since moving to a free-float exchange rate," said Marco Annunziata, chief economist for emerging Europe at Deutsche Bank. \par \par \uc2  Cezary Jozefiak, a member of the Polish Monetary Policy Council, earlier this month reiterated the bank's long-standing view that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not an option. \par \par \uc2  Polish officials believe the market should be left to find its own level and recognise there is little they can do to reverse the trend in the zloty, Annunziata added. \par \par \uc2  In Hungary, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has embarked on an aggressive series of rate cuts which has seen the key two-week deposit rate reduced by 2.5 percent since last May. \par \par \uc2  The cuts are not undertaken to weaken the forint, but justified on the view that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength has helped bring inflation down, offering more scope for monetary easing. \par \par \uc2  "Hungarian policy has been far more accommodative of a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  than the Czechs'," said Juliet Sampson, emerging markets analyst at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  "They're unlikely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  because they're content with the level of the forint, at least for now, and everything they have said confirms that," she added. \par \par \uc2  Sampson said Czech sensitivity stems from a trade and current account balance hit by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength in the past. \par \par \uc2  But she added that there was, as yet, little evidence that strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in emerging Europe were having a major negative impact on exporters. \par \par \uc2  "We're yet to see deficits ballooning, with productivity gains so far helping to offset \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020020122dy1m00tso\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
