\par\par \b \uc2  Polish MPC starts meeting, rates seen flat. \b0\par\par\uc2 451 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2001\par 11:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, Jan 22 (Reuters) - Poland's Monetary Policy Council (MPC) on Monday was holding its first monthly meeting chaired by ex-finance minister Leszek Balercerowicz on Monday, with analysts expecting no interest rate changes to be made. \par \par \uc2  Balcerowicz, who was responsible for Poland's deep economic reforms of the early 1990s, took over the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the chairmanship of the rate-setting Council this month. \par \par \uc2  Most analysts expect the MPC to refrain from cutting interest rates until at least February, when it will know the effects of start-of-the-year increases in controlled prices. \par \par \uc2  Balcerowicz and the 10-member MPC will likely also withhold rate cuts to weigh the effect of a recent government decision to increase the 2001 budget deficit, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Balcerowicz, the leader of a liberal party before his nomination for central banker, will probably also want to establish his image as an independent banker by resisting mounting political pressure for lower rates to rekindle growth. \par \par \uc2  "Judging by MPC members' recent statements, they view January as too early to cut," said Krzysztof Rybinski, chief economist at ING Barings in Warsaw. The MPC's history of missing their end-year inflation targets may also make the hawkish council cautious on cutting the costs of borrowing. The Council aims to lower CPI to 6-8 percent by end-2001 from 8.5 percent in December 2000. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll from Friday showed that analysts expected the MPC to start their long-anticipated process of lowering rates next month, with a 150 basis point cut. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's main interest rate, the 19 percent money market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, stands 10.5 percent above year-on-year inflation. The Lombard rate, a point of reference for many individual credit plans, is at a chokingly high 23 percent. \par \par \uc2  These high rates slowed economic growth to less than three percent in the fourth quarter of 2000 from six percent in the first quarter of last year and froze consumption levels. \par \par \uc2  The zloty, which has steadied after a rapid strengthening in the last months of 2000, is expected to lose ground if rates are unexpectedly cut. \par \par \uc2  If the Council moves to a loosening policy bias from its present neutral stance, the zloty is expected to remain steady. \par \par \uc2  Local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt is expected to remain the focus of international investors even if rates are cut, as the market anticipates a further reduction in rates over 2001. \par \par \uc2  Poland tightened monetary policy in late 1999 to keep a lid on domestic demand and slow a bulging current account deficit, which peaked at 8.3 percent of GDP in March 2000 before narrowing to an estimated 6.2 percent in December. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010712dx1m00m66\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
