\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -ECB euro-buying boosts Europe vs dlr. \b0\par\par\uc2 614 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 2000\par 13:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Nov 3 (Reuters) - Emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Europe rallied strongly against the dollar on Friday, fuelled by a fresh round of European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  euro-buying which propelled the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The zloty and Czech crown both rose more than one percent against the dollar and South Africa's rand also pushed higher after the ECB \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  which sent the euro briefly up more than 1.5 cents to peaks near $0.88. \par \par \uc2  The stronger euro was also marked up against its emerging Europe counterparts but dealers said its gains were relatively small compared with those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' reaction to the retreating dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Emerging Europe has been strengthening generally even against the higher euro, but it is premature to start talking about a big macroeconomic impact on the basis of these fairly small moves in the euro," said one emerging market analyst. \par \par \uc2  A stronger euro is seen benefiting the trade competitiveness of emerging Europe as the bulk of their exports go to euro-zone countries. At the same time, a weaker dollar makes their dollar-denominated imports, particularly oil, cheaper. \par \par \uc2  The zloty firmed to 4.4750 to the dollar, its strongest levels since September 18. It stood at 3.9152 to the euro from early levels around 3.9250. \par \par \uc2  The Czech crown firmed to 39.68/71 per dollar, just short of a two-month peak it hit earlier. It stood at 34.69/72 to the euro from 34.63/38. \par \par \uc2  Analysts added that investor sentiment towards emerging markets was improving, giving \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Europe some independent strength against sharp moves in the euro. \par \par \uc2  "We're seeing better performance in emerging markets in general. JP Morgan's EMBI+ bond index spread over Treasuries has narrowed, stock markets are performing a bit better, conditions are becoming more supportive for emerging markets," said Jake Moore, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital. \par \par \uc2  "But it's too early to say if this is just a temporary lull in the pressures on emerging markets." \par \par \uc2  ZLOTY BUOYED BY CURRENT ACCOUNT DATA \par \par \uc2  The zloty was already marked higher before Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buoyed by \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's better-than-expected current account data for September released late on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The gap came in at a tame $594 million in September as a slowing economy curbed imports and the country delayed the bulk of its foreign debt servicing costs to October, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The deficit was lower than analysts' forecasts as well as the $961 million gap in August. \par \par \uc2  "Poland is definitely becoming more attractive but the upside may be limited because of political problems that could erupt at any point. And though the current account deficit is falling, it is still massive," said Barclay Capital's Moore. \par \par \uc2  The slowing economy could also see Polish interest rates - currently 19-23 percent - cut early next year which could erode support for the zloty, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  Data released on Friday showed Hungary's current account posted a 340 million euro deficit in September after a surplus of 176 million euros in August. \par \par \uc2  "The contrast is interesting between Poland and Hungary because now they look to be at opposite ends of the economic cycle," said David Lubin, emerging markets strategist at HSBC. \par \par \uc2  "Poland is slowing down while Hungary is showing increasing signs of overheating...In terms of relative value, it would make sense to get out of the Hungarian bond market and get into the Polish one." \par \par \uc2  Hungary's forint weakened to 1.98 percent above its parity rate within its 4.5 percent wide trading band after the current account data. On Thursday the forint finished at 2.09 percent above parity. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwb302uv6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
