\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Zloty absorbs rate rise, shekel surges. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,158 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2000\par 12:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 31 (Reuters) - The Polish zloty traded in tight ranges on Thursday following an expected rate rise in the previous session, while the Israeli shekel hit multi-month highs amid speculation over the alteration of its trading band. \par \par \uc2  The zloty recovered from four-week lows against the dollar set on Wednesday ahead of a 150-basis-point increase in \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's main reference rate, the minimum 28-day \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, to 19.0 percent. \par \par \uc2  High interest rates were likely to continue to attract investors into Poland, so boosting the zloty, despite ongoing worries about inflation, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "We have not seen the peak in the interest rate cycle," said Stuart Brown, head of emerging markets research at BNP Paribas in London. "We had a rather hawkish statement from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and Poland already has some of the best levels of return in emerging markets." \par \par \uc2  Monetary Policy Council (MPC) members said the hike was a preventive measure to offset a possible rise in inflationary expectations. They said it would not have a significant effect on economic growth and the zloty. \par \par \uc2  The zloty was trading at 4.3800/4.3815 at 0945 GMT, from Wednesday's lows of 4.4060. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro , the zloty edged off the previous day's one-week highs as the euro recovered slightly across the board, and was trading at 3.9205/28. \par \par \uc2  SHEKEL BREAKS KEY RESISTANCE \par \par \uc2  The shekel firmed to its highest level since March, strengthening beyond the key level of 4.00 to the dollar, as Israeli economic policymakers weighed changing its trading band mechanism and companies converted the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pay September 1 wages. \par \par \uc2  A longstanding debate between the Bank of Israel and the Finance Ministry on the shekel's trading band appeared to soften this week. While the ministry opposes removing the band, sources said it has proposed changing the slope of the band, most probably from a two percent incline to zero. \par \par \uc2  The shekel has inched its way in recent months towards the strongest limit of the band - a mechanism that allows the shekel to fluctuate against a basket of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  some 19 percent above and below a gradually depreciating midpoint. \par \par \uc2  Traders in Israel said that as of early Thursday, the shekel was 1.4 percentage point away from the strongest point in its band and that talk of changing the mechanism emboldened customers to sell more dollars. At current rates, the shekel would need to advance to 3.9330 to the dollar to reach its strongpoint. \par \par \uc2  The shekel retreated beyond 4.00 to the dollar by 0945 GMT from earlier highs of 3.9980, and was trading at 4.0030/70. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seeks to abolish the band and eliminate the need for market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the shekel hits its upper or lower limits. But faced with strong opposition at the Treasury, the Bank of Israel reportedly suggested changing the slope from a two percent depreciation rate to allow for a shekel appreciation of up to three percent yearly. \par \par \uc2  "The trading band discussion is the result of other things which have helped the shekel - higher inflows, falling inflation," said Paul Kinghorn, emerging markets \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC in London. "But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to accompany any change in the band with a rate cut, so we would recommend being long the basket against the shekel." \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Israel said on Monday it would cut its key lending rate by 0.2 of a percentage point to 8.9 percent for September, the eighth reduction in the last 10 months. \par \par \uc2  The Czech crown recovered from the previous day's three-month lows against the dollar in line with a recovery in the euro. \par \par \uc2  All 21 analysts polled by Reuters said the Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not move its two-week repo rate, currently at 5.25 percent, following its board meeting later in the day. \par \par \uc2  The crown was trading at 39.510/535 to the dollar and 35.357/387 to the euro , near earlier one-week lows against the euro of 35.408. \par \par \uc2  Czech end-July money supply, measured by the M2 indicator, grew by 7.3 percent year-on-year after growth of 5.7 percent at the end of June. \par \par \uc2  The Slovak crown largely ignored Wednesday's widely expected cut in key interest rates by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBS).The NBS cut its overnight rates for sterilising and refinancing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  by 50 basis points each to 6.5 percent and 9.5 percent respectively. It cut its two-week repo rate to 8.25 percent, from 8.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  The Slovak crown also benefited from a recovery in the euro, pulling back from the previous day's three-month lows against the dollar to trade at 47.649/719 . It was rangebound against the euro at 42.681/761. \par \par \uc2      ** Following is a table of key emerging market exchange \par \par \uc2  rates, including the change versus late New York trade the \par \par \uc2  previous day and the change in the year to date. For the latest \par \par \uc2  live prices click on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  RICs. \par \par \uc2      ** The Czech, Polish, Hungarian and Slovak \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \par \par \uc2  formerly quoted against the mark, are now quoted against the euro \par \par \uc2  to reflect new market norms. All other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are quoted \par \par \uc2  against the dollar. \par \par \uc2      ** According to market convention, exchange rates are quoted \par \par \uc2  inunits per dollar or euro and the percentage change denotes the \par \par \uc2  rise or fall of the dollar or euro value. \par \par \uc2      ** Annual percentage changes are calculated from late on the \par \par \uc2  first day of the current trading year, January 3. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab \tab     LATEST  PREV    PCT CHG  PCT CHG\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab   CLOSE\tab \tab IN 2000\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPE/MEAST/AFRICA\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Czech Rep.\tab \tab   35.36   35.34   0.05%    -2.00%\par Poland\tab \tab \tab 3.92    3.91    0.09%    -6.56%\par Hungary\tab \tab      261.19  261.03  0.06%     2.67%\par Slovakia\tab \tab     42.68   42.35   0.77%     0.98%\par Romania\tab \tab      22,980  22,890  0.39%    27.24%\par Ukraine\tab \tab      5.44    5.44    0.03%     4.62%\par South Africa\tab \tab 6.95    6.95    0.00%    13.44%\par Kenya\tab \tab \tab  77.00   77.40  -0.52%     5.84%\par Israel\tab \tab \tab 4.000   4.001  -0.02%    -2.51%\par Turkey\tab \tab \tab 652,540 651,550 0.15%    21.08%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par LATAM\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Mexico\tab \tab \tab 9.18    9.19   -0.02%    -2.30%\par Brazil\tab \tab \tab 1.8220  1.8220  0.00%     0.16%\par Venezuela\tab \tab    687.50  687.50  0.00%     5.97%\par Chile\tab \tab \tab  561.50  561.45  0.01%     6.41%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIA\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Singapore\tab \tab    1.719   1.721  -0.10%     3.82%\par Hong Kong\tab \tab    7.799   7.800  -0.01%     0.29%\par China\tab \tab