\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS- \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  history backs call to end shekel trading bands. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Shinichi Kishima \par \uc2 790 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 August 2000\par 17:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Aug 21 (Reuters) - The history of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading bands backs up the view of Bank of Israel governor David Klein who is calling for the bands which confine the shekel to be scrapped while times are good. \par \par \uc2  The central banker told Reuters in an interview on Sunday that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current trading limits could endanger Israel's inflation target and called for their elimination while the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market was calm. \par \par \uc2  Analysts agreed, saying the European exchange rate mechanism (ERM) crisis in 1992 and the Asian crisis which broke out five years later highlighted the difficulties of defending a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  linkage in the face of financial market scepticism. \par \par \uc2  By contrast, the Polish zloty, which went into a free float on April 11, was seen offering encouragement for a transition when market conditions were favourable. \par \par \uc2  The shekel is allowed to trade some 17 percent above and below a gradually depreciating midpoint, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  committed to defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  once it reaches the upper or lower limits of the band. \par \par \uc2  The band depreciates two percent annually. But the shekel has strengthened to within three percent of the top of its band on the back of a strong domestic equity market, investment flows into its technology sector and healthy export growth. \par \par \uc2  Once the top of the band is reached, the BOI would have to buy foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect the peg. This would result in shekels pouring into the private sector banking system, a development which would be inflationary, as Klein pointed out. \par \par \uc2  "The crawl in any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  leads to predictability and that leads to the perception of reduced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks, which in turn leads to increased inward investment," said Callum Henderson, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at SSSB/Citibank in London. \par \par \uc2  "The long-term risk of any crawl is that you attract such large amounts of inward investment because of the predictability that that could lead to a substantially overvalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which eventually is reversed and can, like in the case of Thailand, be reversed quite suddenly with nasty results." \par \par \uc2  POLAND'S MOVE SEEN A SUCCESS \par \par \uc2  Despite worries that a shift to free float could trigger a runaway appreciation in the already buoyant shekel and hurt Israel's trade competitiveness, analysts said the country might be wise to remove the band, which looked certain to be tested. \par \par \uc2  "I think the case for moving to a free float is pretty clear and the Bank of Israel has a strong argument for moving in that direction, for asking the government to go along," said Riccardo Barbieri, head of emerging markets economic research at Morgan Stanley Dean Witter in London. \par \par \uc2  "At the moment, the issue is the stronger side of the band. If the shekel gets there, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be forced to effectively ease interest rates perhaps beyond what they deem appropriate - they want to gain maximum flexibility." \par \par \uc2  Britain's experience of sterling being forced out of the ERM in 1992, highlighted the economic damage that could result from using monetary policy to defend \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading bands. \par \par \uc2  The British authorities jacked up interest rates in a bid to fend off speculative attack on the pound, even though tighter monetary policy was not what the economy needed. \par \par \uc2  But this only prompted speculators, who did not think higher rates were sustainable, to step up their attack on sterling. \par \par \uc2  To be sure, there is little evidence Israel's economy is overheating, and there has even been speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would cut interest rates in the near future anyway. \par \par \uc2  "At some point they will have to ease interest rates irrespective of the exchange rate target because they're overachieving in terms of inflation. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to have maximum flexibility to hit the inflation target also on a medium-term basis," Barbieri said. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Poland's recent shift to free float has been a success overall, despite some initial market volatility. \par \par \uc2  "Poland adopted a very prudent policy of eliminating the crawl while the market was quite calm in order to avoid a situation whereby it would be forced to abandon the crawl during more volatile and turbulent times," Henderson said. \par \par \uc2  Were \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  forced to defend its old peg through \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it would have been in more serious trouble in attempting to contain inflation, they said. \par \par \uc2  Polish inflation data last week showed consumer prices had risen by 11.6 percent in July, well above expectations and spurring talk of a near-term interest rate increase of one percentage point or more. \par \par \uc2  "The inflation picture is a little bit different in Israel, but the long-term risk is the same," said a trader at a U.S. bank in London. \par \par \uc2 Dokumen