\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Zloty falls to fresh lows as confidence fades. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Justyna Pawlak \par \uc2 526 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 1999\par 18:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par WARSAW, Nov 3 (Reuters) - The Polish zloty\tab   fell sharply on Wednesday as mounting worries over Poland's macroeconomy and political rifts within the government coalition triggered selling in a thin market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market talk that the reform-minded Finance Minister Leszek Balcerowicz had resigned - later denied by government officials - sparked the first wave of sales, which were exacerbated by stop-loss trades, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  At 1712 GMT the zloty traded at 3.82 percent weaker than its trading band centre, against intraday lows around four percent and Wednesday's average fixing of 2.62 percent. It last traded at such levels in July 1997. \par \par \uc2  It was at 4.2835 to the dollar and at 4.4970 to the euro. \par \par \uc2  "First, rumours that Balcerowicz had resigned fuelled a mild panic. Even though the talk was refuted, some players had to realise stop-losses and the negative sentiment persisted," said Mateusz Bieniek, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Bank Handlowy in Warsaw. \par \par \uc2  The rumours of Balcerowicz's departure arose despite the coalition's late Tuesday agreement on a tax reform introduced by the Finance Minister, which ended a heated debate within the government and eased worries of a political crisis. \par \par \uc2  The junior coalition partner Freedom Union (UW), headed by Balcerowicz, said it had accepted a less radical version of its tax reform proposal put forward by the Solidarity Election Action (AWS). \par \par \uc2  MACROECONOMIC WORRIES PREVAIL \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the plunge signalled the high level of foreign investors caution about the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to worries over runaway inflation and widening current account deficit. It was part of a now-established downtrend, they said. \par \par \uc2  Member of the rate-setting Monetary Policy Council (RPP) Wieslawa Ziolkowska told Reuters the zloty's plunge was "unjustified" because the Polish economy remained sound. \par \par \uc2  "The Polish economy has sound foundations. Industrial output is rising. Cross-border trade data suggests exports may finally rebound. This is not the right time for such reactions...This unjustified move should be short-lived," she said. \par \par \uc2  Investor caution over Poland's economy started after a steep CPI pickup in recent months, which forced the Finance Ministry to increase its end-year inflation forecasts from around seven percent to some eight percent. \par \par \uc2  In September inflation rose to a year-on-year eight percent, forcing the RPP to give up on its end-year CPI target of 6.6-7.8 percent. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the National Bank of Poland called them for zloty rates several times during the day, which some said signalled the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  considered \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's goal is to limit inflation. Protecting the current account deficit and growth is the responsibility of the government," Ziolkowska said when asked about the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect the zloty. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current account gap widened to $1.115 billion in September from August's $786 million and the deficit is expected to reach seven percent of gross domestic product by end-year. ((Warsaw Newsroom, tel. +48 22 653 9700, fax. +48 22 653 9781, warsaw.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba000