\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW-Polish CPI trend a dilemma for C.bank. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jan Strupczewski \par \uc2 718 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 1999\par 13:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, Oct 27 (Reuters) - Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  faces a tough choice between raising interest rates in response to rising inflation or trusting price growth will slow in the second half of 2000, senior finance ministry adviser Stanislaw Gomulka said. \par \par \uc2  Gomulka said the year-on-year inflation index would likely inch up well into the first half of next year because the statistical base of the second half of 1998 and early 1999 was the lowest since 1991. \par \par \uc2  "But for the same reason, the year-on-year index will start falling sharply in the second half of next year, because the base of the second half of this year is high," he told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  "However, in the meantime, the Monetary Policy Council (RPP) will be under enormous pressure to raise rates because of growing inflation expectations, which translate into phenomena such as wage growth in the real economy," he said. \par \par \uc2  "This is a genuine economic argument for raising interest rates," he added. \par \par \uc2  The more than six month delay in the transmission of monetary policy changes into the real economy would mean that any rate rise would take effect only when inflation was already again falling, he said. \par \par \uc2  "Interest rates should not be a reaction to what is happening, but an anticipation of what will happen with inflation, and not just for the next few months," Gomulka said. \par \par \uc2  "But the RPP cannot ignore the psychological factor, so if the perception is that there is something wrong with inflation and that the credibility of the council is in danger, then they will be under enormous pressure (to raise rates)." \par \par \uc2  Gomulka said year-on-year inflation could rise to between eight and nine percent in December from eight percent in September and 8.6 percent in December 1998. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wanted to bring inflation down to 6.6-7.8 percent this year. \par \par \uc2  The index dropped to a low of 5.6 percent in February as a result of an economic slow-down in the wake of the Russian financial crisis in the second half of 1998. \par \par \uc2  The RPP raised its main market rate by 100 basis points to 14 percent in September but held off from a further rise this month. \par \par \uc2  CURRENT ACCOUNT GAP MAY RISE \par \par \uc2  Gomulka said Poland's high current account deficit, which may reach seven percent of gross domestic product this year against 4.3 percent in 1998, may be even higher next year, because of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy of stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. \par \par \uc2  "If we have a flexible exchange rate regime with stable reserves, the deficit on the current account has to be equal to the surplus on the capital account, which is currently around seven percent of GDP," he said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the Polish \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this year and stopped trading \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with commercial banks at the daily fixing session in June as part of its policy to prepare to freely float the zloty in 2000. \par \par \uc2  "Next year the capital account surplus may rise, maybe a percentage point or so," Gomulka said. \par \par \uc2  "So even if there are better external conditions and exports start growing it will not help the current account because it (the capital account) will also affect the exchange rate and imports - its an economic axiom," he said. \par \par \uc2  Gomulka said that if, for instance, the capital account surplus reached levels of 12-15 percent of GDP, \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  should change its exchange rate regime and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market to keep the current account gap at relatively safe levels. \par \par \uc2  "This is only contingency thinking, because we do not expect that kind of capital inflows," he said. \par \par \uc2  What will determine a safe deficit level is the quality of the capital account surplus - if it was mainly valuable foreign direct investment (FDI) or volatile portfolio flows. \par \par \uc2  "If we see that we have more FDI, we can allow ourselves a higher current account deficit and keep the flexible exchange rate," he said. \par \par \uc2  The government estimates foreign direct investment this year at $7.0 billion and forecasts next year's FDI at $7.5 billion. ((Warsaw Newsroom, tel.+48 22 653 9700, fax. +48 22 653 9781, warsaw.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1999. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dvar012qk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
