\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Czech  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  tussle marks emerging Europe's week. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jan Lopatka \par \uc2 981 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 1999\par 18:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  PRAGUE, Oct 8 (Reuters) - The Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (CNB) moves to knock down the buoyant crown \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dominated Prague financial markets, while Warsaw and the Baltics led gainers in emerging Europe equity trading this week. \par \par \uc2  The CNB abruptly ended speculation about how far it would let the crown appreciate on the back of strong foreign direct investment (FDI) inflows by a surprise rate cut on Monday followed by two heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the ruling coalition was locked in debates on reshaping the unpopular cabinet, but the equity market jumped on improved investor sentiment, following no change in interest rates in the eurozone and the United States. \par \par \uc2  CZECH \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  SHOOTS AT CROWN \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  shocked the market on Monday when its monthly monetary meeting, expected to bring no news, resulted in a 25 basis point cut in the two-week repo rate to 5.75 percent quickly followed by heavy selling of crowns on the open market. \par \par \uc2  The first Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since July 1998 knocked the crown 2.5 percent lower to 36.85 to the euro. Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought hundreds of millions of euros. The bank struck again on Thursday, selling crowns at around 36.45. \par \par \uc2  The cabinet and the CNB have said the crown was too strong and may harm exports, the main source of economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The CNB also said it had agreed in principle with the Finance Ministry to propose unspecified measures to the full cabinet to tame the effects of the FDI inflow, expected to rise with a wave of privatisations planned for the next year. \par \par \uc2  The crown closed the week at around 36.60 to the euro. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, better prospects for banking privatisation and a firming of telecom stocks, put Prague stock indices more than two percent higher on the week, as equities largely ignored the turmoil on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  POLAND REBOUNDS DESPITE POLITICAL TROUBLES \par \par \uc2  Polish shares rebounded this week on improved global sentiment after more than a month of steep falls, but analysts said the recovery was half-hearted. \par \par \uc2  The rise came as investor confidence improved, despite continued worries about Poland's internal political situation and a recent run of poor macroeconomic data. \par \par \uc2  Poland's fractious ruling coalition is locked in discussions to reshape the cabinet in a bid to improve its dismal popularity ratings. Talk of dismissing the prime minister or a government collapse have diminished as the week went on. \par \par \uc2  The market began to slide on Friday, after the all-share WIG index peaked at just below the 15,000-point resistance level on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said Friday's positive U.S. employment data may persuade some foreign investors back into the market. \par \par \uc2  The zloty lingered just below the centre of its 30-point trading band throughout the week mired by an unclear outlook for Poland's economy after better current account gap figures took the edge off worse-than-feared inflation numbers. \par \par \uc2  HUNGARY HIGHER IN SLOW TRADE \par \par \uc2  Hungarian shares gained over two percent this week but traders said that despite the gains - which followed a run of losses in recent weeks - cash flows by foreign investors, who usually account for the bulk of turnover, remain miniscule. \par \par \uc2  Fears of the impact of the Y2K computer bug on markets and worries of a potential rate hike in the United States ahead of Fed meeting late on Tuesday weighed on sentiment, they said. \par \par \uc2  "There's not a lot of money flowing this way," said Balazs Bence, trader at Cashline Securities. "This uncertainty on the American market is likely to remain...(and) because of this year 2000 problem a lot of people think investors are waiting the market out and that things will only pick up again in January." \par \par \uc2  OTHER MARKETS \par \par \uc2  * Baltic shares were higher across the board, with investors focusing on Lithuanian bank issues after leading bank Vilniaus' takeover of rival Hermis for $58 million got final clearance from the competition authority. \par \par \uc2  * Slovenian SBI share index almost unchanged this week amid poor liquidity and little foreign interest. \par \par \uc2  * Turnover and volumes fell on the Bulgarian Stock Exchange in five trading sessions in the week ending Friday as real-time trade was light and only one block deal was sealed. \par \par \uc2  Total weekly turnover fell to 694,922 levs ($380,400) from 1.87 million levs in five sessions in the previous week as weekly traded volume fell to 172,663 units from 213,769. \par \par \uc2  * Croatian stocks rose almost five percent before giving back some of the gains on Friday as two separate German investments helped improve the country's poor image with the international financial community. \par \par \uc2  * The Bucharest bourse slipped after state-run commercial bank Banca Agricola lost ground in the week to Friday on high liquidity following a share split, traders said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab     CLOSE    WEEK'S CHANGE  1999 HIGH  1999 LOW\par \tab \tab     OCT 8\tab NET    PCT\par  WARSAW (WIG)\tab  14,854.3  472.2    3.3  17,645.9    12,591.1\par  BUDAPEST (BUX)\tab 6,830.98 191.43   2.88  7,625.75    5,130.26\par  PRAGUE (RHNW)\tab  1,417.9   31.4    2.26  1,562.5\tab  920.2\par  BUCHAREST(BET-C)\tab 522.28  -8.92  -1.67    611.32\tab 425.22\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  LJUBLJANA (SBI)     1,888.48  -7.45  -0.4   1,991.32    1,679.48\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  BRATISLAVA (RJT)\tab 921.84  37.36   4.22  1,020.3\tab  508.7\par  RIGA (RICI)\tab      158.61   7.55   5.00    191.97\tab 151.06\par  LITHUANIA (LITIN10) 1,308.96  98.01   8.09  1,343.62\tab 967.98\par  TALLINN (TALG)\tab    99.44   0.26   0.26    123.25\tab  91.34\par  ZAGREB (Crobex)\tab  540.2   16.1    2.68    803.0\tab  508.7.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ((Prague newsroom, +420-2-2419-0477, fax +420-2-2422-9935,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par prague.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dva802clk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
