\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Czech  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  battle marks emerging Europe's week. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jan Lopatka \par \uc2 895 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Oktober 1999\par 13:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  PRAGUE, Oct 8 (Reuters) - The Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's (CNB) repeated moves to knock down the buoyant crown \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dominated Prague financial markets, while Warsaw led emerging Europe's gains in equity trading this week. \par \par \uc2  The CNB abruptly ended speculation about how far it would let the crown appreciate on the back of strong foreign direct investment (FDI) inflows by a surprise rate cut on Monday followed by two heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  In \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the ruling coalition was locked in debates on reshaping the unpopular cabinet, but the equity market jumped on improved investor sentiment, following no change in interest rates in the eurozone and the United States. \par \par \uc2  CZECH \b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  SHOOTS AT CROWN \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  shocked the market on Monday when its monthly monetary meeting, expected to bring no news, resulted in a 25 basis point cut in the two-week repo rate to 5.75 percent quickly followed by heavy selling of crowns on the open market. \par \par \uc2  The first Czech \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since July 1998 knocked the crown 2.5 percent lower to 36.85 to the euro. Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought hundreds of millions euro. \par \par \uc2  The cabinet and the CNB have said the crown was too strong and may harm exports, the main source of economic recovery. The crown started recovering prompting the bank to strike again on Thursday, selling crowns at around 36.45 to the euro. \par \par \uc2  The CNB also said it had agreed in principle with the Finance Ministry to propose unspecified measures to the full cabinet to tame the effects of the FDI inflow, expected to rise with a wave of privatisations planned for the next year. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, better prospects for banking privatisation and a firming of telecom stocks, put Prague stock indices more than two percent higher on the week, as equities largely ignored the turmoil on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. \par \par \uc2  POLAND REBOUNDS DESPITE POLITICAL TROUBLES \par \par \uc2  The Warsaw bourse rebounded this week after more than a month of steep falls on improved worldwide sentiment and relief that major international interest rates were kept on hold. \par \par \uc2  The four percent increase came as investor confidence improved, despite continued worries about Poland's internal political situation and a recent run of poor macroeconomic data. \par \par \uc2  Poland's fractious ruling coalition is locked in discussions to reshape the cabinet in a bid to improve its dismal popularity ratings. Talk of dismissing the prime minister or a government collapse have diminished as the week went on. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The zloty\tab   lingered just below the centre of its 30-point trading band throughout the week mired by an unclear outlook for Poland's economy after better current account gap figures took the edge off worse-than-feared inflation numbers.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par HUNGARY EDGES HIGHER IN SLOW TRADE\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Hungarian shares gained over one percent this week but traders said that despite the gains - which followed a run of losses in recent weeks - cash flows by foreign investors, who usually account for the bulk of turnover, remain miniscule.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Fears of the impact of the Y2K computer bug on markets and worries of a potential rate hike in the United States ahead of Fed meeting late on Tuesday weighed on sentiment, they said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There's not a lot of money flowing this way," said Balazs Bence, trader at Cashline Securities. "This uncertainty on the American market is likely to remain...(and) because of this year 2000 problem a lot of people think investors are waiting the market out and that things will only pick up again in January."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     OTHER MARKETS\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Baltic shares were slightly higher across the board, with\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par investors focusing on Lithuanian bank shares after the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par competition authority approved the merger of leading bank\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Vilniaus  with rival Hermis .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Slovenian SBI share index\tab     almost unchanged this\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par week amid poor liquidity and little foreign interest.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     * Block deals at pre-agreed prices boosted turnover and\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par volumes on the Bulgarian Stock Exchange in five trading sessions\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par in the week ending on Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    * Croatian stocks gained almost five percent on the week as\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par two separate German investments helped improve the country's\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par poor image with the international financial community.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      Blue chip Zagrebacka Banka    rose on news German\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par insurer Allianz was mulling acquiring a five percent stake.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Drugs firm  rose on optimism sparked by news that the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par state would get $850 million for the sale of 35 percent of the\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par state telecom company to Deutsche Telekom .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \tab \tab \tab    CLOSE    WEEK'S CHANGE  1999 HIGH  1999 LOW\par \tab \tab \tab OCT 7\tab NET    PCT\par BUDAPEST (BUX)\tab 6,724.05   84.5    1.27   7,625.75  5,130.26\par WARSAW (WIG)\tab  14,970.9   588.8    4.09  17,645.9  12,591.1\par PRAGUE (RHWN)\tab  1,415.5    29.0    2.1    1,562.5     920.2\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par LJUBLJANA (SBI)     1,893.91   -2.02  -0.1    1,991.32  1,679.48\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BRATISLAVA (RJT)\tab 923.58   39.1    4.42   1,020.3     508.7\par RIGA (RICI)\tab      159.30    2.69   1.72     191.97    151.06\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par LITHUANIA (LITIN10) 1,343.62  132.77  10.97    1297.66    967.98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TALLINN (TALG)\tab    99.18    0.06   0.06     123.25     91.34\par ZAGREB (Crobex)\tab  549.7    23.6    4.49     803.0     508.7\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  ((Prague newsroom, +420-2-2419-0477, fax +420-2-2422-9935,\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par prague.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010909dva802any\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
