\par\par \b \uc2  PRESS DIGEST - Poland - June 29. \b0\par\par\uc2 443 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Juni 1999\par 11:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1999 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  Here are highlights from Polish newspapers on Tuesday. Reuters has not verified these stories and does not vouch for their accuracy. \par \par \uc2  ECONOMIC AND MARKET STORIES RZECZPOSPOLITA - Restructuring in the domestic coal-mining sector is going on according to its schedule, deputy Economy Minister Jan Szlazak said. The Minister denied that Poland plans to make radical cuts in coal miner employment to liquidate coalmines. The government plans to cut 30,000 workers by the end of 1999. - The U.S. agency for commerce and development and the commercial section of the U.S. embassy have allocated nearly 716,000 dollars to conduct feasibility studies of three investments in power engineering industry, telecommunication and municipal sectors. - In the second quarter of 1999, Polish families considered economic condition of their households as worse than that in the same period last year. Poles expect higher unemployment but generally they are more optimistic, said economic development institute at Warsaw School of Economics (IRG SGH). GAZETA WYBORCZA - South Korea will start lifting customs barriers for Poland's foodstuffs, Korean Prime Minister Kim Jong Pil said. - The national power grid Polskie Sieci Energetyczne ranked fifth on a Wall Street Journal list of the largest businesses in Central and Eastern Europe. Poland's largest telecoms Telekomunikacja Polska SA (TPSA) ranked eighth on the list. PRAWO I GOSPODARKA - Observance of public budget discipline was worse in 1998 than the year before, said chief executive officer at the Supreme Auditing Chamber (NIK), Janusz Wojciechowski in his evaluation of the 1998 budget performance. NIK criticised three ministries the health ministry, the \b \highlight22\uc2 treasury ministry\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the culture ministry. PARKIET - BIG Bank Gdanski, the major shareholder in meat plant Sokolow SA with nearly 20 percent of votes, has failed to place its representative in the Sokolow SA's supervisory board. - A subsidiary of Pernod Ricard has submitted a statement to Consumer and Competition Protection Office (UOKiK) declaring its willingness to buy registered shares providing 74 percent of votes in Agros form Ireland's subsidiaries of TIGA. PULS BIZNESU - The National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  (NBP) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Monday to limit overly high daily fluctuations of the Polish zloty value. It is the first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  since August 1998. - Ferry operator Polska Zegluga Baltycka will invite investors to participate in its non-public share issue this July. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Looking for more information from local sources? Reuters Business Briefing has seven Polish sources including Rzeczpospolita. For details of the product please call your local help desk\tab \tab  .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ((Warsaw Newsroom, tel. 48 22 653 9700, fax. +48 22 653 9781, warsaw.newsroom@reuters.com)).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (C) Reuters Limited 1999.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010907dv6t05gb7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
