\par\par \b \uc2  INSTANT VIEW - Polish RPP cuts interest rates. \b0\par\par\uc2 769 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 1998\par 18:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, Oct 28 (Reuters) - Poland's Monetary Policy Council (RPP) lowered the lombard rate by two percentage points to 22 percent and the main 28-day market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate by one percent to a minimum of 17 percent as of October 29. \par \par \uc2  The RPP cut the discount rate by 1.5 percentage points to 20.0 percent and also widened the zloty's daily fluctuation band to plus/minus 12.5 percent from the parity rate from the previous plus/minus 10 percent limit. \par \par \uc2  Following are analysts' reactions to the decisions: \par \par \uc2  WIKE GROENENBERG, ECONOMIST AT SALOMON, SMITH & BARNEY \par \par \uc2  "The decision to lower interest rates doesn't come as a surprise. It reflects the sharp decline in inflation over the last few months, the slowing in the economic growth rate, as well as the firming of the zloty. What is somewhat surprising is that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also decided to widen the band. I think they are doing this because they want to create more scope for the zloty to appreciate. Poland has become an area of interest for investors as they are betting on longer-term convergence. \par \par \uc2  "A cut of 100 basis points was very much discounted, so I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will remain firm or to strengthen further. In the longer run the monetary authorities want to increase the risk premium for foreigners investing in Poland and widening the band is part of this policy." \par \par \uc2  ZSOLT PAPP, SBC WARBURG \par \par \uc2  "I think it was definitely a good move. If you look at the economic data, it certainly justified the decision to cut interest rates. Real interest rates are still relatively high, so there is no actual risk. If you look at the situation on financial markets, it seemed to justify it too. The zloty has been strengthening quite sharply over the last few weeks. It was probably the right time to take some pressure out of it. \par \par \uc2  "Because everybody was expecting the rate cut, if they hadn't cut now, it could have backfired. The rate cut should have been largely priced in, but I think in the short term the zloty could even strengthen further, because the decision signals confidence. The economy is growing, inflation is coming down, the current account deficit is not a problem, they are also not that afraid of external impact. The band widening that comes with it is also a signal that the national bank is preparing for more convertibility of the zloty. But in the longer run the zloty should probably ease again over the next few months." \par \par \uc2  SONJA GIBBS, NOMURA, LONDON \par \par \uc2  "I do think at least here in London many people were looking for a little bit more off the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate, particularly in light of all the indicators in \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2  which have really supported the case for lower interest rates. A little bit of disappointment there on the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate and prospects of continued zloty strength. \par \par \uc2  "I think the widening in the band was perhaps expected a little bit futher on, but the zloty has been seeing quite a lot in terms of capital inflow, which has really been supporting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the point where plus/minus 10 percent starts to look as if it was not quite enough, especially on the upside. So by doing this and by not quite cutting the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate as much as it has been expected what you probably see is more zloty strength pushing toward the upper end of this range. \par \par \uc2  "The case for rate reduction in Poland is so clear...that we could look for more cuts there, probably another 200 basis points before the end of the year." \par \par \uc2  JOHN LOMAX, ANALYST AT HSBC JAMES CAPEL \par \par \uc2  "I think it's moderately good news in the sense that the key 28-day rate was cut pretty much in line with expectation. The lombard and the discount rates were cut more agressively. As important is the widening of the trading band to 12.5 percent. The aim there seems to have been two-fold. First, it should deter speculative capital from coming into Poland, and secondly, the widening of the band actually gives the RPP some scope to keep real interest rates higher for longer without running into any exchange rate constraints. \par \par \uc2  "I imagine the stock market will take the news moderately well, not hugely well, since a lot of this had already been discounted after the cabinet approved the budget." \par \par \uc2  ((Warsaw Newsroom, tel. +48 22 653 9700, \par \par \uc2  fax +48 22 653 9780, warsaw.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926duas05v1e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
