\par\par \b \uc2  Russia crisis will not change Polish policy - RPP. \b0\par\par\uc2 185 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 1998\par 19:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  WARSAW, Sept 9 (Reuters) - Poland's Monetary Policy Council (RPP) said on Wednesday it would not change the course of its interest rate and \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy because of the crisis in Russia. \par \par \uc2  "Taking into consideration Poland's solid fundamentals, the RPP believes it is justified to continue the present course of changes in monetary policy, including exchange rate and interest rate policy," \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Hanna Gronkiewicz-Waltz told a news conference. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Poland\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision-making RPP cut its 28-day market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate to a minimum of 18 percent from the earlier floor of 19 percent. \par \par \uc2  It also slowed the rate of zloty's monthly devaluation to 0.5 percent against a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket from the previous 0.65 percent a month. \par \par \uc2  Over the last six months the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's RPP council has repeatedly lowered both market and official interest rates and slowed the pace of the zloty's devaluation. ((Wojciech Moskwa, Warsaw Newsroom, tel. +48 22 653 9700, fax +48 22 653 9780, warsaw.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du9900r4w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
